00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:02.050
It's not too difficult to understand why so many Americans are going hungry.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:07.480
It comes down to a few factors. The big one is the economic factor.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.659
Millions of people are being thrown out of work all around the world.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.289
And so it means that they just don't have the money to be able to afford to eat.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.610
In the ways that they used to do.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:19.779
When millions of Americans start losing their jobs and the economy begins to suffer,.

00:02:20.469 --> 00:02:21.800
hunger isn't far behind.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:29.889
What we learned from the 2008 recession is that food insecurity is a lagging indicator.

00:02:30.909 --> 00:02:34.110
when the kind of economic devastation that we've experienced occurs.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.980
And even though the economy as the economy, I should say, recovers,.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.310
Food insecurity lags behind.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:51.089
So we did not see food security return to the pre 2008 recession levels until about 2018.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.918
Despite the economic catastrophe covid has created,.

00:02:54.196 --> 00:03:00.040
one of the more striking facts about food insecurity is that it doesn't actually need to exist.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:04.569
Unlike other periods of that you might think of with a large famine,.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.889
we actually don't have a situation of insufficient supply.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.569
So that's what really makes this breaking right now.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.970
There's no shortage of food in the world.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.889
There's more than enough food to feed every single person on the planet.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.509
As the pandemic hit, there are stories that seem to defy logic.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:28.180
Why are farmers all across the country breaking eggs, slaughtering cattle and dumping milk?

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:33.101
While grocery store shelves were empty and lines for food banks went on for blocks.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.770
A lot of food in the country was directed straight to restaurant demand and straight to the food sector,.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.350
and redirecting that from restaurants and into grocery stores was very difficult to do.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:50.289
The food supply chain that gets food from a farm onto a person's plate in America.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.439
Is extremely efficient, but it's also extremely fragile.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:57.729
Usually it goes from the farmer to a packager.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:01.280
Where it gets packaged in separate amounts depending on the customer.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:06.039
Those amounts can be drastically different, depending on who's buying the food,.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.149
Which became a problem when restaurants started closing en masse.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.729
You think about the example of butter.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:17.739
You may have, you know, 40 pound crates of butter going into restaurants.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:23.148
And obviously nobody wants to bring 40 pounds of butter home into their personal homes.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:29.859
And so the the way that that food gets transferred is supplied and sent through the supply chain has become very narrow in the U.S.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:37.009
And it was difficult for farmers to adjust from one situation to another.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:43.350
So the same efficiency that keeps prices down during pre-pandemic times threw everything into chaos.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.660
But some farmers have been able to adjust by bypassing traditional supply chains altogether.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:53.949
The other thing that people have done in me in recent months.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:59.170
Was actually going from farmers, going directly to consumers in a bigger way.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:06.279
A lot of farms started turning their own farm parking lot or some pickup location.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:14.019
Into a place where they could take advanced online orders and offer pickup or some limited form of delivery.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:20.470
We followed with our software in helping them work with individuals,.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:27.730
As you know, both for them to make up for the missed sales from restaurants and schools,.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:35.839
but also for them to offer individuals a way to shop from a farm that didn't involve going to a store.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:42.309
So for some Americans, going to farms directly does help alleviate some of the supply chain pressures during a crisis.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:48.579
But it doesn't solve the problem for millions of Americans, which is they're unable to buy food at all.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:56.259
Our biggest challenge right now is there are a few federal programs that have really helped us.

